\begin{abstract}
This project studies whether traditional constant-variance risk models understate downside risk in U.S. state and local government employee pension funded ratios. Public pension plans support the retirement income of tens of millions of Americans and represent a major asset class in the U.S. financial system, with over \$6 trillion in state and local government pension assets. We model pension asset returns using equities, fixed income, real estate, and private equity proxies. We estimate expanding-window GARCH(1,1) models to capture volatility clustering and combine standardized residuals through a multivariate t-copula to model cross-asset tail dependence. We then simulate one-year funded-ratio distributions from 2017 to 2024 and compare 5\% Value-at-Risk and Conditional Value-at-Risk against a constant-variance benchmark. The results suggest that incorporating volatility clustering and nonlinear dependence produces more conservative downside risk estimates, implying that simpler constant-variance models may provide an overly optimistic view of pension solvency risk.
\end{abstract}

