% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}

This project studies funding shortfall risk for U.S. state and local government employee pension plans. These plans are economically important because tens of millions of Americans either currently rely on them or will rely on them in the future for retirement income. Public pension funds are also a significant asset class in the U.S. financial system, with over \$6 trillion in assets for state and local governments. A strong individual example is the New York City Police Department Pension Fund, which alone manages over \$60 billion in assets. As a result, pension solvency is not only a retirement-security issue, but also a large-scale financial risk-management issue.

A pension plan's financial condition is commonly summarized by its funded ratio,

\begin{equation}
FR_t = \frac{A_t}{L_t},
\end{equation}

where $A_t$ represents plan assets and $L_t$ represents plan liabilities. A higher funded ratio indicates stronger solvency, while a lower funded ratio signals greater pressure on future contributions, benefits, or investment performance.

The main risk is that asset losses may occur exactly when volatility is elevated and diversification breaks down. Standard constant-variance models assume stable volatility and relatively stable asset-class dependence. However, financial markets often display volatility clustering, meaning that large shocks are followed by periods of elevated uncertainty. Asset classes may also become more dependent during stress periods. These features are especially important for pension funds because left-tail events can materially reduce funded ratios.

The central research question is:

\begin{quote}
\textit{Do constant-variance models understate downside funded-ratio risk relative to a GARCH-copula framework?}
\end{quote}

To answer this, we compare a GARCH-copula simulation model against a constant-variance benchmark. The GARCH model captures time-varying volatility within each asset class, while the t-copula captures dependence across asset-class shocks, including possible joint tail behavior.

% ============================================================
\section{Data}

The pension data come from the Public Plans Dataset from Boston College. The dataset includes quarterly time-series data for almost 250 major state and local government pension plans and covers roughly 95\% of both pension plan membership and pension plan assets. The sample is split roughly evenly between state and local pension plans. The pension data include annual assets, liabilities, funded ratios, contribution and benefit flows, investment return assumptions, and asset allocation weights.

Because asset and liability holdings are reported annually, we fit return models to asset-class representative ETFs in order to obtain monthly return data. The market data consist of monthly adjusted-close returns from four exchange-traded funds used as proxies for major pension asset classes.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Asset-Class Proxies}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Asset Class & Proxy & Description \\
\midrule
Equities & SPY & U.S. equity market exposure \\
Fixed Income & IEF & Intermediate-term Treasury exposure \\
Real Estate & VNQ & Real estate investment trust exposure \\
Private Equity & PSP & Listed private equity exposure \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Monthly log returns are computed from adjusted closing prices. The model is estimated using expanding windows, with each test year from 2017 to 2024 using only data available through the prior year. This avoids look-ahead bias when estimating the risk model for each test year.

The weighted-average asset allocation shows that pension funds have gradually moved toward real estate and private equity while reducing exposure to equities and fixed income. This shift is important because private equity and real estate exposures may increase sensitivity to illiquidity, delayed valuation, and joint downside movement during market stress.

