\section{Methodology}

For each simulation path, assets evolve according to the simulated portfolio return and net cash flows:

\begin{equation}
A_{t+1} = A_t(1 + R_{p,t+1}) + NF_{t+1},
\end{equation}

where $R_{p,t+1}$ is the simulated portfolio return and $NF_{t+1}$ represents net contributions and benefit payments. The portfolio return is computed as:

\begin{equation}
R_{p,t+1} =
w_{EQ}R_{EQ,t+1} +
w_{FI}R_{FI,t+1} +
w_{RE}R_{RE,t+1} +
w_{PE}R_{PE,t+1}.
\end{equation}

Liabilities are revalued using a duration-based approximation:

\begin{equation}
L_{t+1} = L_t
\left(
\frac{1+y_t}{1+y_t+\Delta y_t}
\right)^{D_L},
\end{equation}

where $y_t$ is the initial discount-rate assumption, $\Delta y_t$ is the implied change in yield, and $D_L$ is liability duration. Implied yield changes are inferred from fixed-income returns using IEF as the fixed-income proxy.

Each asset class is modeled using a GARCH(1,1) process with skewed Student-t errors:

\begin{equation}
r_t = \mu + \epsilon_t, 
\qquad
\epsilon_t = \sigma_t z_t,
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\sigma_t^2 =
\omega +
\alpha \epsilon_{t-1}^2 +
\beta \sigma_{t-1}^2.
\end{equation}

The parameter $\alpha$ captures the short-run volatility response to shocks, while $\beta$ captures volatility persistence. When $\alpha+\beta$ is close to one, volatility shocks decay slowly, which is important for modeling pension downside risk after market stress.

After fitting the GARCH models, we transform the standardized residuals into pseudo-observations and fit a multivariate t-copula:

\begin{equation}
C_{\nu, R}(u_1, u_2, u_3, u_4),
\end{equation}

where $R$ is the dependence matrix and $\nu$ is the degrees-of-freedom parameter. The t-copula is useful because it can capture tail dependence, meaning that extreme losses across asset classes may occur together more often than a normal correlation model would imply.

For each test year from 2017 to 2024, we estimate the model using data through the prior year, simulate 10,000 one-year monthly return paths, aggregate returns annually, update assets and liabilities, and compute the simulated funded ratio. We then estimate 5\% Value-at-Risk and Conditional Value-at-Risk:

\begin{equation}
VaR_{5\%} = q_{0.05}(FR),
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
CVaR_{5\%} =
E[FR \mid FR \leq VaR_{5\%}].
\end{equation}

Because lower funded ratios are worse, lower VaR and CVaR values indicate greater downside risk. The constant-variance benchmark uses the same asset-liability simulation structure, but simulates returns using constant historical means and standard deviations instead of GARCH-based conditional volatility and copula dependence.

As an alternative robustness approach, we also consider a PCA-GARCH model. PCA rotates correlated asset returns into uncorrelated principal components, GARCH models are fitted to each component independently, and simulated components are rotated back into asset returns. This approach captures time-varying volatility while preserving the correlation structure through the PCA rotation matrix.

