Across the test years, clustered volatility gives more conservative VaR and CVaR estimates than the constant-volatility assumption. The mean VaR gap is approximately 4.79 funded-ratio points, while the mean CVaR gap is approximately 7.39 funded-ratio points. The largest gaps are 8.48 points for VaR and 12.01 points for CVaR, showing that the constant-variance model can materially understate downside funded-ratio risk in stressed periods.

Figure \ref{fig:distribution} compares simulated funded-ratio distributions for 2021. The GARCH-copula model places more probability mass in the left tail, meaning that it assigns greater likelihood to adverse funded-ratio outcomes.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.88\textwidth]{figure5.png}
\caption{Simulated Funded-Ratio Distribution, 2021: GARCH-Copula vs. Constant Variance}
\label{fig:distribution}
\end{figure}

The PCA-GARCH robustness check produces a similar qualitative conclusion. The first principal component behaves like a broad market factor, loading positively on risky assets and explaining most of the portfolio variance. This implies that pension portfolios may be less diversified than they appear when viewed only through asset-class labels. PCA-GARCH also shows high volatility persistence and a drift in the equity-fixed-income relationship toward zero, reinforcing the finding that the historical bond hedge weakened around the 2022 rate-hiking cycle.

% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion and Implications}

The results suggest that constant-variance models can understate pension downside risk by smoothing over volatility clustering and failing to capture nonlinear dependence across asset classes. Even if two models produce similar average funded ratios, they can imply very different left-tail outcomes. For pension plans, the left tail is the most important region because it represents scenarios in which assets fall materially short relative to liabilities.

The GARCH-copula framework improves on the benchmark by modeling both time-varying volatility and cross-asset tail dependence. This produces more conservative funded-ratio VaR and CVaR estimates, which may offer a stronger basis for stress testing, asset allocation, and contribution planning.

There are still limitations. The ETF proxies do not perfectly replicate actual pension holdings, especially private equity and real estate. Asset volatility for real estate and private equity may also be overstated because reported pension returns are often smoothed. The liability model is simplified because liabilities are anchored to reported values and adjusted using a fixed duration, rather than modeled as a fully stochastic actuarial process. A Bayesian informative prior was tested for liabilities but did not produce a significant improvement, likely because only 24 liability observations were available. Finally, the static copula may not fully capture changing crisis-period dependence, which may explain why the copula model produces somewhat more optimistic tail-risk estimates than the PCA-GARCH alternative. Even with these limitations, the results support the main conclusion: constant-variance models may provide an overly optimistic view of pension solvency risk.

